<Base title="Sources"
  subtitle="What has been read, and what has only been identified"
  lede="Named so that every claim on this site can be traced back, and so that the gap between what is actually held and what is merely known to exist stays visible.">

  <h2>Read, and cited</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Yad Vashem, Central Database of Shoah Victims&rsquo; Names</strong> (collections.yadvashem.org) — searched properly on 17 September 2026, having previously been used for one list only. LEIBHOLZ returns <strong>88 victim records</strong> across the List of Murdered Jews from Germany, the <strong>List of Deportation from Berlin</strong>, <strong>eighteen Pages of Testimony</strong> and the Theresienstadt inmate list. <strong>Jacob&rsquo;s record (13520936) names the train: &ldquo;Transport 30 from Berlin to Auschwitz Birkenau on 26/02/1943&rdquo;</strong>, and gives his citizenship as German. <strong>Oskar&rsquo;s (13449776)</strong> gives Theresienstadt 17 March 1943, Auschwitz 16 October 1944, and death the same day. It also identifies a source this archive had been quoting at second hand without knowing it: the <strong><em>Gedenkbuch Berlins der jüdischen Opfer des Nationalsozialismus</em></strong>, Freie Universität Berlin, Edition Hentrich, Berlin 1995 — <em>Berlin&rsquo;s own memorial book, not the national one</em>, and the origin of the transport number.</li>
    <li><strong>Arolsen Archives</strong> (collections.arolsen-archives.org) — the international centre on Nazi persecution, some 30 million documents referring to around 17.5 million people. <strong>Index read 17 September 2026; the documents were not opened.</strong> 213 person entries under LEIBHOLZ. It confirms, from a body of records entirely separate from the Bundesarchiv, <strong>JAKOB LEIBHOLZ 08.08.1884 SCHUBIN</strong>, <strong>OSKAR LEIBHOLZ 13.05.1887 SCHUBIN</strong> and <strong>SIEGFRIED LEIBHOLZ 05.08.1925</strong>; it carries two of the brothers under the compulsory name — <em>LEIBHOLZ, ISRAEL OSKAR</em> and <em>LEIBHOLZ, GEORG ISRAEL</em>; it holds a post-war <strong>tracing case for Oskar, T/D 52.971, fourteen documents</strong>; and it holds <strong>Kurt himself</strong>. <strong>The documents stop here on purpose:</strong> opening one requires ticking a declaration of consent about the use of sensitive personal data, which is a statement a named person makes about their own conduct, and <em>this archive does not tick it on anybody's behalf</em>. Everything cited is from the index, which needs no consent.</li>
    <li><strong>The National Archives, <em>Discovery</em></strong> (discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk) — the catalogue of Kew <em>and of more than two thousand five hundred other British archives</em>. Free, no account, answers JSON. <strong>First used on 17 September 2026, after two hundred and nineteen commits that cited Kew references without once opening the catalogue they belong to.</strong> Every citation on this site verifies, and two verify more precisely than they were published: <strong>WO 392/15</strong> is specifically <em>&ldquo;Section 5: South Africa (Union Defence Forces)&rdquo;</em>, which is exactly the claim made here about Leonard Wear; <strong>WO 329/1120</strong> adds the page range 312–622. It also gave <strong>WO 372/4/50790</strong>, the item reference for Frederick James Castle&rsquo;s medal card, and <strong>DP/12/1/14</strong> and <strong>DP/12/1/15</strong>, the Baldock baptism registers at Hertfordshire Archives. <em>LUWINSKI returns zero</em> across every archive it describes. Wrapped in <code>tools/tna.py</code>, which has a <code>--verify</code> mode. <strong>On 17 September that mode was run over the whole site: forty-four distinct archival references, forty-two verified exactly, two resolved after one correction, and none wrong, missing or misattributed.</strong> The two were the same mistake twice and it is not really ours — <code>RG 15/7079</code> and <code>RG 14/7576</code> carry a <strong>leading zero, which is how Findmypast prints a piece number and not how Kew does</strong>. The catalogue also confirmed the <em>content</em>: RG 11/574 is <em>5K St James Bermondsey</em>, HO 107/1560 is <em>Bermondsey (Street Indexed)</em>, RG 14/10304 is <em>Colchester South-East</em>, and the three BT 27 pieces are Southampton Feb–Mar 1937, London Apr–Jun 1939 and London Aug–Oct 1939 — Leonard’s sailing, his father’s and his mother’s, in the right ports in the right months.</li>
    <li><strong>Zentral- und Landesbibliothek Berlin, <em>Berliner Adressbücher</em></strong> (digital.zlb.de) — the Berlin city directories, <strong>digitised annually from 1799 to 1970</strong>, six to seven thousand pages a volume, listing householders by <strong>name, trade and street</strong>. Free, full-text, JSON. Read from 17 September 2026; wrapped in <code>tools/zlb.py</code>. <strong>1,047 page hits for LEIBHOLZ and 23 for LUWINSKI.</strong> The counts by year, with controls, are in <code>data/berlin-adressbuch.tsv</code>. <em>The OCR is Fraktur and the columns jam</em> — counting is reliable, reading a named line needs the page image.</li>
    <li><strong>Findmypast</strong>, full subscription, from 16 September 2026 — the 1851, 1871, 1881, 1891, 1901, 1911 and 1921 censuses of England and Wales; the 1939 Register; the GRO birth, marriage and death indexes; BT 27 outbound passenger lists; <em>Soldiers Died in the Great War</em>; the First World War campaign medal rolls and medal index cards; WO 363 and WO 364 service records; <em>British Armed Forces and Overseas</em> births, marriages and deaths; and Hertfordshire Baptisms. Most of the Wear and Castle work on this site is from it. <strong>Its transcripts are not the records</strong>: the 1911 barracks return that names Frederick Castle&rsquo;s regiment has an empty occupation column in the transcript and reads <em>Scottish Rifles</em> on the image.</li>
    <li><strong>FamilySearch full-text search</strong>, signed in, 17 September 2026 — which reads the words on unindexed images rather than a name index. LUWINSKI, LUWINSKY, LUWENSKI and the phrase <em>Piccadilly Hat</em>: thirty-three hits and <strong>every one American</strong>. The control ran first and the corpus really does hold South Africa — but what it holds is the voters&rsquo; rolls of <strong>1925–1931</strong>, and every person this archive wants arrived after the last roll was printed.</li>
    <li><strong>gendatabase.com</strong> — a free mirror of the South African archives index with death and estate databases attached, and it does stem matching where the official index does not. It answered in four seconds a question that had taken a day, and produced three Luwinski court files nobody had seen.</li>
    <li><strong>Bundesarchiv, <em>Gedenkbuch — Opfer der Verfolgung der Juden unter der nationalsozialistischen Gewaltherrschaft in Deutschland 1933–1945</em></strong>, online edition, read 14 September 2026. Entries <a href="https://apps.bundesarchiv.de/gedenkbuch/en1100957">en1100957</a> (Jacob), <a href="https://apps.bundesarchiv.de/gedenkbuch/en909753">en909753</a> (Samuel), <a href="https://apps.bundesarchiv.de/gedenkbuch/en1100894">en1100894</a> (Oskar), <a href="https://apps.bundesarchiv.de/gedenkbuch/en1100801">en1100801</a> (Georg). Also the surname search returning 50 Leibholz entries, which is what showed the two family clusters.</li>
    <li><strong>MyHeritage</strong>, Defranceski family site, family tree 9 — 164 individuals and 648 facts, read 14 September 2026. The spine of this archive and the thing most of the work consists of checking.</li>
    <li><strong>MyHeritage DNA</strong> — ethnicity estimate and match list for Lynn Derrick Luwinski's kit, read 14 September 2026.</li>
    <li><strong>FamilySearch</strong>, <em>South Africa, Civil Marriage Records, 1840–1973</em> — Kurt Luwinski and Ivone de Figueiredo de Witt, 19 June 1948, Johannesburg, certificate 858 (<a href="https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:6ZHT-WSJM">1:1:6ZHT-WSJM</a>; page image <a href="https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-CSJ1-SJQK">3:1:3Q9M-CSJ1-SJQK</a>, digital film 008109682, frame 2047 — read from the film on 15 September 2026, having twice been read only from a photograph in the family tree); Morris Isaac Salkow and Ivone Luwinski, 13 February 1953 (<a href="https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:6ZHR-B6Y3">1:1:6ZHR-B6Y3</a>). Hanover, Orange Free State records, for the 1956 marriage — but see the next entry: the citation is the film group's catalogue title, not the contents of the frame.</li>
    <li><strong>Marriage register, entry No. 169 of 1956</strong>, form B.M.D. 3 (a) — <em>Kurt Luwinski and Frances Sefton, formerly Berner, born Hostovsky, 29 February 1956, Johannesburg, Transvaal</em>. Held in the MyHeritage tree as photo 9500001, catalogued there as <em>record-image_</em> and attached to Frances; original scan 3,918 × 5,245 px, read at full resolution 14 September 2026. Every field transcribed in <code>notes/marriage-register-1956.md</code>. The frame is plainly Johannesburg, whatever the film group is called.</li>
    <li><strong>MyHeritage Y-DNA</strong>, kit MH-7GGV62, read 14 September 2026 — haplogroup <strong>R-S11477</strong>, reported as nested within R-Z381, haplogroup family R1b/Z381, parent node R-FGC13328; with the Country Frequency table for 74 testers. MyHeritage labels its own call a <em>low-resolution estimate</em> (microarray, not Big Y), and the Y-DNA pages are powered by FamilyTreeDNA. Phylogeny and frequencies for R-U106/Z381, and Ashkenazi Y-haplogroup frequencies, from the published literature — see <code>notes/ydna-result.md</code> for the working.</li>
    <li><strong>Gazettes.Africa</strong> (Laws.Africa / AfricanLII) — a free, <strong>full-text searchable</strong> archive of African government gazettes; <strong>54,313 South African</strong> issues. A search for <em>Luwinski</em> returns eight, giving the Piccadilly Hat Company notices of 1940–52 and two for Pauline in 1972 and 1975. Transcribed in <code>notes/piccadilly-hat-company.md</code>. <strong>Its Mozambique run begins only in 1975</strong>, so the colonial <em>Boletim Oficial</em> is not there.</li>
    <li><strong>FamilyTreeDNA Discover</strong> (public, no account), haplogroup <strong>R-S11477</strong> — ancestral path, age estimates, the 42 downstream branches, and the Suggested Projects listing that names the surname projects on the branch. Read 14 September 2026.</li>
    <li><strong>MyHeritage DNA Matches</strong>, Derrick's kit, <em>name or ancestral surname</em> search across all <strong>5,464</strong> matches, 14 September 2026. Results in <code>notes/surname-sweep.md</code>. Match holders are living and are described by role, country and shared centimorgans only.</li>
    <li><strong>National Archives of South Africa (NAAIRS)</strong>, read 14 September 2026 — <em>at an address that has since moved.</em> The search system now answers at <code>www.national.archsrch.gov.za/sm300cv/smws/</code>; the old host stopped resolving on 16 September and this archive briefly published that the service was down, which was wrong. On 17 September it answered <strong>503</strong> — a server saying it exists and cannot reply, which is a different thing again, and nothing has been published from it since. <strong>All 43 documents indexed under FIGUEIREDO</strong>, saved to <code>data/naairs-figueiredo.tsv</code> — among them <strong>WLD 1950/1948</strong>, which gives Ivone's birth name in full. Also the seven under LUWINSKI, including the estate file <strong>TAB MHG 7584/69</strong>, and the single document under the variant spelling <strong>LUWINSKY</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Stolpersteine Berlin</strong> — checked for Leibholz stones; those found (Willmanndamm 6, Danckelmannstraße 44) belong to other Leibholz families.</li>
  </ul>

